# Java Conventions

## Dependency Injection

Use constructor injection, not `@Inject` on fields. Mark dependencies `final`.

```java
// Good
@ApplicationScoped
public class MyService {
    private final OllamaApiService ollamaApiService;

    public MyService(final OllamaApiService ollamaApiService) {
        this.ollamaApiService = ollamaApiService;
    }
}

// Bad
@ApplicationScoped
public class MyService {
    @Inject
    OllamaApiService ollamaApiService;
}
```

## Prefer `final`

- Method parameters: `final String text`
- Local variables: `final var result = ...`
- Fields: `private final OllamaApiService ollamaApiService`

## Records for DTOs

Use Java records for data transfer objects. Place them in the `dto/` or `dto/requests/` sub-package of the relevant domain package (see `package-structure` rule). Cross-cutting inference results go in `api.dto.inferenceresults/`.

Inference result records use `@InferenceDescription` (from `com.chriswininger.ollama` in the shared `ollama-client` library) on fields — not LangChain4j's `@Description` — so `OllamaApiService` can build format blocks for structured output. `OllamaApiService` is provided via `OllamaApiServiceProducer`; inject it by type, do not instantiate directly.

## Two-Pass AI Service Pattern

Services that call the LLM follow a two-pass approach:

1. **Unstructured pass** -- plain-text system prompt, returns free-form analysis via `callOllamaPlainTextResponse`
2. **Structured pass** -- takes the plain-text output and converts it to a record via `callOllamaStructuredResponse`, using `buildExampleJson(RecordClass.class)` in the system prompt

Prompts are `private static final String` text blocks at the top of the service class.

## CDI Beans

- Services are `@ApplicationScoped`
- Resources (JAX-RS endpoints) use `@Path`

## Logging

Use `org.jboss.logging.Logger` with `LOG.infof(...)` format-string style.

```java
private static final Logger LOG = Logger.getLogger(MyService.class);
```

## Text Blocks

Use text blocks (`"""`) for multi-line strings such as prompts and user messages. Use `.formatted(...)` for interpolation and `.trim()` where needed.

## LangChain4j API Patterns

The project uses LangChain4j 1.x (`dev.langchain4j:langchain4j`). Key API details:

### AiServices — simple singleton (no tools)

Use `AiServices.create()` when the service has no per-call state. Produce it as a CDI singleton.

```java
AiServices.create(MyAiService.class, chatModel);
```

### AiServices — with tools (per-call state)

Use `AiServices.builder()` when tools are bound per invocation. Create a factory bean instead of a singleton.

```java
AiServices.builder(MyAiService.class)
        .chatModel(model)          // NOT .chatLanguageModel()
        .tools(toolInstance)
        .maxSequentialToolsInvocations(10)
        .build();
```

### Key annotations by package

| Annotation | Package | Usage |
|---|---|---|
| `@Tool` | `dev.langchain4j.agent.tool` | Marks a method as an LLM-callable tool |
| `@P` | `dev.langchain4j.agent.tool` | Describes a tool method parameter |
| `@SystemMessage` | `dev.langchain4j.service` | System prompt on an AI service interface method |
| `@UserMessage` | `dev.langchain4j.service` | User message template on an AI service interface method |
| `@V` | `dev.langchain4j.service` | Binds a method parameter to a template variable |
